    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Strata-bound zones of sigmoidal veins (SVZs) are prevalent throughout siliceous shale reservoirs of the southern San Joaquin Valley, California. Siliceous shales of the Monterey Formation have extremely low permeabilities. Consequently, we attempted to determine, using extensive field and laboratory observations, the role that sigmoidal veins assume in enhancing hydrocarbon production from fields that draw from Monterey Formation reservoirs. A mini-permeameter study indicated that sigmoidal veins are one to two orders of magnitude more permeable than the surrounding siliceous shale host rock. Increased permeabilities result from secondary deformational features that form along vein margins and serve as conduits for hydrocarbon migration. Thus, SVZs create strata-bound networks of fluid flow pathways which are observed to be connected to deeper petroleum sources via high-angle normal faults and joint zones. By establishing the impact that SVZs have on hydrocarbon production in the southern San Joaquin Valley, our knowledge may be extended to other Monterey Formation and equivalent siliceous shale reservoirs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
